AVM: play through the default output audio unit, and record the microphone

QEMU's coreaudio backend opened the default output device with an IOProc
and set the device's own stream format to the guest's rate. When the
device refused that rate it played nothing: AirPods in hands-free mode run
at 24 kHz and refuse 48 kHz (2026-10-27). It reopened only when the
default device changed, not when the same device changed rate, and setting
the device's format changed it for every other app, the Mac's VoiceOver
included.

This plays through kAudioUnitSubType_DefaultOutput instead. The unit
converts QEMU's fixed rate to the device's, follows the default output and
its rate changes by itself, and leaves the device's format alone. The
buffer settings keep their meaning: out.buffer-length is the device's
buffer for this process, converted to the device's rate, and the ring
holds out.buffer-count of what each render asks for, plus one spare for a
device that drops to a lower rate mid-session.

Recording (2026-10-27): the backend had none (max_voices_in 0). It now
records the Mac's default microphone through a HAL output unit with only
its input side on, at the microphone's own rate and channel count; QEMU's
audio core converts to the guest's. The microphone is opened when the
guest starts recording and closed when it stops, so macOS's microphone
indicator is on only then, and the default microphone is looked up each
time.
Sound the guest hasn't produced yet plays as silence. AudioToolbox joins
CoreAudio in the backend's frameworks.

